{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "name": "Request payload silently dropped when retransmitting Uri-Path-Abbrev requests (coap_net.c)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Request payload silently dropped when retransmitting Uri-Path-Abbrev requests (coap_net.c)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When a CoAP session has learned that the peer does not understand the Uri-Path-Abbrev option, `coap_send()` rebuilds outgoing requests with the abbreviation expanded. The rebuilt PDU is created correctly, but the original message payload is appended to the *old* PDU, which is then freed, so the request that reaches the wire carries no payload. Any body-bearing PUT/POST issued on such a session is delivered to the server without its content."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "When a CoAP session has learned that the peer does not understand the Uri-Path-Abbrev option, `coap_send()` rebuilds outgoing requests with the abbreviation expanded. The rebuilt PDU is created correctly, but the original message payload is appended to the *old* PDU, which is then freed, so the request that reaches the wire carries no payload. Any body-bearing PUT/POST issued on such a session is delivered to the server without its content.\n\nIntegrity of client requests is silently altered: a request issued with content is transmitted without any content. For a resource whose handler treats an empty body as \"replace with nothing\" (or which deletes on empty PUT), the effect is data loss on the server; for any other handler the request semantics change without the application being informed. In a proxy chain the truncated request is relayed upstream as-is, so the same loss propagates. No confidentiality or availability impact was demonstrated.\n\nAdd the request payload to the duplicated PDU that is actually transmitted, not to the source PDU that is deleted immediately afterwards. The duplicated PDU deliberately carries only the token and options, so the payload must be re-added explicitly after the duplication succeeds and before the source PDU is released. Consider adding a regression test that sends a body-bearing request through the Uri-Path-Abbrev fallback and asserts the payload arrives, since no existing unit test covers this branch.",
                "markdown": "When a CoAP session has learned that the peer does not understand the Uri-Path-Abbrev option, `coap_send()` rebuilds outgoing requests with the abbreviation expanded. The rebuilt PDU is created correctly, but the original message payload is appended to the *old* PDU, which is then freed, so the request that reaches the wire carries no payload. Any body-bearing PUT/POST issued on such a session is delivered to the server without its content.\n\nIntegrity of client requests is silently altered: a request issued with content is transmitted without any content. For a resource whose handler treats an empty body as \"replace with nothing\" (or which deletes on empty PUT), the effect is data loss on the server; for any other handler the request semantics change without the application being informed. In a proxy chain the truncated request is relayed upstream as-is, so the same loss propagates. No confidentiality or availability impact was demonstrated.\n\nAdd the request payload to the duplicated PDU that is actually transmitted, not to the source PDU that is deleted immediately afterwards. The duplicated PDU deliberately carries only the token and options, so the payload must be re-added explicitly after the duplication succeeds and before the source PDU is released. Consider adding a regression test that sends a body-bearing request through the Uri-Path-Abbrev fallback and asserts the payload arrives, since no existing unit test covers this branch."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.7",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-770",
              "name": "Unauthenticated memory exhaustion in OSCORE Appendix B.2 context handling",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated memory exhaustion in OSCORE Appendix B.2 context handling"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When an OSCORE security context has RFC 8613 Appendix B.2 enabled, libcoap derives and permanently links a new OSCORE security context for every incoming request that carries a previously unseen `kid_context`, and it does so **before** the AEAD tag is verified. When verification fails (always, for a peer without the master secret) the newly created context is never removed. An unauthenticated attacker can therefore make the server allocate an unbounded number of security contexts by sending requests with ever-changing `kid_context` values, eventually exhausting memory and taking the CoAP service down."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "When an OSCORE security context has RFC 8613 Appendix B.2 enabled, libcoap derives and permanently links a new OSCORE security context for every incoming request that carries a previously unseen `kid_context`, and it does so **before** the AEAD tag is verified. When verification fails (always, for a peer without the master secret) the newly created context is never removed. An unauthenticated attacker can therefore make the server allocate an unbounded number of security contexts by sending requests with ever-changing `kid_context` values, eventually exhausting memory and taking the CoAP service down.\n\nRemote, unauthenticated denial of service. Each crafted datagram permanently adds one OSCORE security context (master secret/salt copies, sender and recipient contexts, derived keys) to the CoAP context chain. Memory grows without bound and is never reclaimed until the process restarts; because context lookup walks the whole chain, request processing cost also degrades quadratically as the chain grows. On the constrained devices libcoap targets, a few thousand packets are enough to exhaust the available heap.\n\nTreat the Appendix B.2 derived security context as provisional until the request that caused its creation has been authenticated: record the context created by the duplication, keep it only when AEAD verification succeeds, and otherwise release the session reference and remove it from the CoAP context chain. Alternatively, rate-limit or cap the number of security contexts that can be created from unauthenticated traffic, or defer context creation until the child context can be validated. Deployments that do not need RFC 8613 Appendix B.2 should keep `rfc8613_b_2` disabled.",
                "markdown": "When an OSCORE security context has RFC 8613 Appendix B.2 enabled, libcoap derives and permanently links a new OSCORE security context for every incoming request that carries a previously unseen `kid_context`, and it does so **before** the AEAD tag is verified. When verification fails (always, for a peer without the master secret) the newly created context is never removed. An unauthenticated attacker can therefore make the server allocate an unbounded number of security contexts by sending requests with ever-changing `kid_context` values, eventually exhausting memory and taking the CoAP service down.\n\nRemote, unauthenticated denial of service. Each crafted datagram permanently adds one OSCORE security context (master secret/salt copies, sender and recipient contexts, derived keys) to the CoAP context chain. Memory grows without bound and is never reclaimed until the process restarts; because context lookup walks the whole chain, request processing cost also degrades quadratically as the chain grows. On the constrained devices libcoap targets, a few thousand packets are enough to exhaust the available heap.\n\nTreat the Appendix B.2 derived security context as provisional until the request that caused its creation has been authenticated: record the context created by the duplication, keep it only when AEAD verification succeeds, and otherwise release the session reference and remove it from the CoAP context chain. Alternatively, rate-limit or cap the number of security contexts that can be created from unauthenticated traffic, or defer context creation until the child context can be validated. Deployments that do not need RFC 8613 Appendix B.2 should keep `rfc8613_b_2` disabled."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.9",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-770",
                  "stride:D"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-476",
              "name": "NULL pointer dereference in OSCORE external credential lookup crashes the server",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "NULL pointer dereference in OSCORE external credential lookup crashes the server"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When OSCORE credentials are supplied through the external credential callback API, `oscore_find_context()` can return a security context while leaving the caller's recipient-context pointer NULL if none of the recipients in the returned credential set matches the key id from the request. The OSCORE request path uses that returned context without checking the recipient pointer and dereferences NULL, crashing the CoAP process. A single unauthenticated OSCORE packet with an attacker-chosen key id triggers it."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "When OSCORE credentials are supplied through the external credential callback API, `oscore_find_context()` can return a security context while leaving the caller's recipient-context pointer NULL if none of the recipients in the returned credential set matches the key id from the request. The OSCORE request path uses that returned context without checking the recipient pointer and dereferences NULL, crashing the CoAP process. A single unauthenticated OSCORE packet with an attacker-chosen key id triggers it.\n\nA single unauthenticated datagram can terminate the CoAP server process (NULL pointer dereference), denying service to all clients until it is restarted; libcoap has no isolation between sessions, so the whole service goes down. No credentials or prior state are required, but the target application must be using the external credential callback API with a credential store that can return a recipient set not containing the requested key id.\n\nMake the external-credential lookup fail closed: when the credential set returned by the callback contains no recipient matching the requested key id, discard the temporary context and return NULL so the caller answers 4.01 instead of continuing with a NULL recipient context. Applications using the external credential API should also ensure their store returns a credential set that contains the requested recipient id, and should treat unknown key ids as a lookup miss rather than returning a partial context.",
                "markdown": "When OSCORE credentials are supplied through the external credential callback API, `oscore_find_context()` can return a security context while leaving the caller's recipient-context pointer NULL if none of the recipients in the returned credential set matches the key id from the request. The OSCORE request path uses that returned context without checking the recipient pointer and dereferences NULL, crashing the CoAP process. A single unauthenticated OSCORE packet with an attacker-chosen key id triggers it.\n\nA single unauthenticated datagram can terminate the CoAP server process (NULL pointer dereference), denying service to all clients until it is restarted; libcoap has no isolation between sessions, so the whole service goes down. No credentials or prior state are required, but the target application must be using the external credential callback API with a credential store that can return a recipient set not containing the requested key id.\n\nMake the external-credential lookup fail closed: when the credential set returned by the callback contains no recipient matching the requested key id, discard the temporary context and return NULL so the caller answers 4.01 instead of continuing with a NULL recipient context. Applications using the external credential API should also ensure their store returns a credential set that contains the requested recipient id, and should treat unknown key ids as a lookup miss rather than returning a partial context."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.9",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-476",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-457",
              "name": "Uninitialised heap memory disclosed through re-assembled Block1 body when the block size grows mid-transfer",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Uninitialised heap memory disclosed through re-assembled Block1 body when the block size grows mid-transfer"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`libcoap` re-assembles a Block1 request body into a heap buffer but never initialises the bytes the client did not send. A client can force such a gap by increasing the SZX (block size) between blocks: the write offset of the next block is computed from the new SZX (`num << (szx + 4)`), so it jumps past the data already received, while the received-block bookkeeping still treats the transfer as complete. The application is then handed a body of `total_len` bytes in which the gap holds whatever was previously in that heap memory. With the example server's echo mode the leaked bytes are returned to the client in the 2.01 response."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`libcoap` re-assembles a Block1 request body into a heap buffer but never initialises the bytes the client did not send. A client can force such a gap by increasing the SZX (block size) between blocks: the write offset of the next block is computed from the new SZX (`num << (szx + 4)`), so it jumps past the data already received, while the received-block bookkeeping still treats the transfer as complete. The application is then handed a body of `total_len` bytes in which the gap holds whatever was previously in that heap memory. With the example server's echo mode the leaked bytes are returned to the client in the 2.01 response.\n\nAn unauthenticated client that can send UDP or TCP CoAP requests obtains heap contents it never supplied: on a non-sanitised build, 38 of 40 attempts returned previously freed request payloads and heap/library pointers inside the 2.01 response body, and the same buffer is handed to any application using `COAP_BLOCK_SINGLE_BODY` or the public `coap_block_build_body()` helper. The leaked region is attacker-sized (up to the configured maximum body size) and repeatable, so it can be used to read freed heap data such as other peers' request payloads and pointer values that help defeat ASLR. Integrity and availability are not affected by the PoC.\n\nReject a Block1/Q-Block1 request whose SZX differs from the block size already established for that transfer (except for the first block), because the NUM to offset mapping of an in-progress transfer is fixed and a size increase leaves a gap that is never filled. Additionally, never expose never-written bytes: zero the body buffer when it is created and zero the newly added tail whenever the body is grown, so that a gap is defined data rather than heap contents. Both changes are needed — the first enforces the RFC 7959 block-sequence rule, the second guarantees the \"every byte of the delivered body was either supplied by the client or is zero\" invariant for any future path that computes an offset.",
                "markdown": "`libcoap` re-assembles a Block1 request body into a heap buffer but never initialises the bytes the client did not send. A client can force such a gap by increasing the SZX (block size) between blocks: the write offset of the next block is computed from the new SZX (`num << (szx + 4)`), so it jumps past the data already received, while the received-block bookkeeping still treats the transfer as complete. The application is then handed a body of `total_len` bytes in which the gap holds whatever was previously in that heap memory. With the example server's echo mode the leaked bytes are returned to the client in the 2.01 response.\n\nAn unauthenticated client that can send UDP or TCP CoAP requests obtains heap contents it never supplied: on a non-sanitised build, 38 of 40 attempts returned previously freed request payloads and heap/library pointers inside the 2.01 response body, and the same buffer is handed to any application using `COAP_BLOCK_SINGLE_BODY` or the public `coap_block_build_body()` helper. The leaked region is attacker-sized (up to the configured maximum body size) and repeatable, so it can be used to read freed heap data such as other peers' request payloads and pointer values that help defeat ASLR. Integrity and availability are not affected by the PoC.\n\nReject a Block1/Q-Block1 request whose SZX differs from the block size already established for that transfer (except for the first block), because the NUM to offset mapping of an in-progress transfer is fixed and a size increase leaves a gap that is never filled. Additionally, never expose never-written bytes: zero the body buffer when it is created and zero the newly added tail whenever the body is grown, so that a gap is defined data rather than heap contents. Both changes are needed — the first enforces the RFC 7959 block-sequence rule, the second guarantees the \"every byte of the delivered body was either supplied by the client or is zero\" invariant for any future path that computes an offset."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-457",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/457.html"
            },
