      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "vuln-0001",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Request payload silently dropped when retransmitting Uri-Path-Abbrev requests (coap_net.c)\n\nWhen a CoAP session has learned that the peer does not understand the Uri-Path-Abbrev option, `coap_send()` rebuilds outgoing requests with the abbreviation expanded. The rebuilt PDU is created correctly, but the original message payload is appended to the *old* PDU, which is then freed, so the request that reaches the wire carries no payload. Any body-bearing PUT/POST issued on such a session is delivered to the server without its content."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/coap_net.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 1805,
                  "endLine": 1805,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "coap_add_data(pdu, data_len, data);"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Copy the request payload into the duplicated PDU instead of the discarded source PDU"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/coap_block.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 5197,
                  "endLine": 5197,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "coap_add_data(pdu, data_len, data);"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Informational: the equivalent block-layer fallback correctly adds the payload to the duplicated PDU (there `pdu` is the new PDU)"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "coap_send() → coap_net.c (Uri-Path-Abbrev expansion path)",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/coap_net.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 1805,
                        "endLine": 1805
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "coap_add_data(new, data_len, data);"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Add the request payload to the duplicated PDU that is actually transmitted, not to the source PDU that is deleted immediately afterwards. The duplicated PDU deliberately carries only the token and options, so the payload must be re-added explicitly after the duplication succeeds and before the source PDU is released. Consider adding a regression test that sends a body-bearing request through the Uri-Path-Abbrev fallback and asserts the payload arrives, since no existing unit test covers this branch.",
                "markdown": "Add the request payload to the duplicated PDU that is actually transmitted, not to the source PDU that is deleted immediately afterwards. The duplicated PDU deliberately carries only the token and options, so the payload must be re-added explicitly after the duplication succeeds and before the source PDU is released. Consider adding a regression test that sends a body-bearing request through the Uri-Path-Abbrev fallback and asserts the payload arrives, since no existing unit test covers this branch."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "6872784ed4de66fe011e132cc008f7fb89d4b67e214d82d432899f2b42f3d7da"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "3.7",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "da67d6185326bc2574d5aa55afec4906f612653799a41877ea87354abb0f8ba1",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "low",
              "cvss": 3.7,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 11:11:21 UTC",
              "target": "libcoap 4.3.5 (source snapshot)",
              "endpoint": "coap_send() → coap_net.c (Uri-Path-Abbrev expansion path)",
              "method": "CoAP (UDP datagram generated by the library)",
              "impact": "Integrity of client requests is silently altered: a request issued with content is transmitted without any content. For a resource whose handler treats an empty body as \"replace with nothing\" (or which deletes on empty PUT), the effect is data loss on the server; for any other handler the request semantics change without the application being informed. In a proxy chain the truncated request is relayed upstream as-is, so the same loss propagates. No confidentiality or availability impact was demonstrated.",
              "technical_analysis": "`coap_send_internal()` contains a fallback for peers that rejected the Uri-Path-Abbrev option. Once `session->no_path_abbrev` is set (it is set by the library when the server answers `4.02 Bad Option` to an abbreviated request), every subsequent request carrying a Uri-Path-Abbrev option is duplicated with `coap_pdu_duplicate_lkd(..., COAP_BOOL_TRUE)`, which expands the abbreviation into Uri-Path options. That function explicitly copies only the token and options, not the payload:\n\n```c title=coap_pdu.c startLineNumber=233 endLineNumber=235\n/*\n * Note: This does not include any data, just the token and options\n */\n```\n\nThe payload therefore has to be re-added to the *new* PDU. The code instead appends it to the source PDU:\n\n```c title=src/coap_net.c startLineNumber=1802 endLineNumber=1808\n      new = coap_pdu_duplicate_lkd(pdu, session, pdu->actual_token.length,\n                                   pdu->actual_token.s, NULL, COAP_BOOL_TRUE);\n      if (new) {\n        if (coap_get_data(pdu, &data_len, &data)) {\n          coap_add_data(pdu, data_len, data);\n        }\n        coap_log_debug(\"*  Retransmitting PDU with Uri-Path-Abbrev replaced (3)\\n\");\n        coap_delete_pdu_lkd(pdu);\n        pdu = new;\n```\n\n`pdu` is the PDU that is deleted two lines later, so the data is written to freed-and-discarded storage, while `new` (the PDU that is actually transmitted) never receives a payload. The call also emits `coap_add_data: PDU already contains data`, because the source PDU already held that payload.\n\nThe identical construct elsewhere in the library writes to the new PDU and is correct, confirming the intended behaviour:\n\n```c title=src/coap_block.c startLineNumber=5191 endLineNumber=5199\n          pdu = coap_pdu_duplicate_lkd(lg_crcv->sent_pdu, session, ltoken_len,\n                                       ltoken, NULL, COAP_BOOL_TRUE);\n        if (pdu) {\n          if (coap_get_data(lg_crcv->sent_pdu, &data_len, &data)) {\n            coap_add_data(pdu, data_len, data);\n          }\n```\n\nReachability: the fallback runs only for sessions where the library's block layer is active (`session->block_mode & COAP_BLOCK_USE_LIBCOAP`) and `session->no_path_abbrev` has been set, which requires the application to use the Uri-Path-Abbrev facility (`coap_path_into_optlist_abbrev()` / `coap_uri_into_optlist_abbrev()` with a mapping). A remote CoAP server (or an on-path attacker able to rewrite server responses) can force the transition by answering `4.02` to the first abbreviated request, after which every abbreviated body-bearing request from that client is silently sent without its payload.",
              "remediation_steps": "Add the request payload to the duplicated PDU that is actually transmitted, not to the source PDU that is deleted immediately afterwards. The duplicated PDU deliberately carries only the token and options, so the payload must be re-added explicitly after the duplication succeeds and before the source PDU is released. Consider adding a regression test that sends a body-bearing request through the Uri-Path-Abbrev fallback and asserts the payload arrives, since no existing unit test covers this branch.",
              "counterevidence": "Looked for a reason the payload might legitimately be re-added elsewhere: `coap_pdu_duplicate_lkd()` documents and implements \"token and options only\" (no data), so no other code path restores it; the sibling implementation at `src/coap_block.c:5197` adds the payload to the duplicated PDU, which produces a correct request. Considered whether the data might already live inside the duplicate: it does not, and the observed wire bytes on the vulnerable build contain no payload. Considered whether the path is unreachable: it requires libcoap block mode plus the Uri-Path-Abbrev feature (a library API not used by the shipped examples), which limits exposure; that constraint is reflected in the attack complexity and in the low severity, but it does not make the behaviour intended. No memory-safety consequence was observed, only loss of the payload.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If Uri-Path-Abbrev were in common use (or enabled by default for body-bearing requests), or if a downstream resource treated an empty PUT as a destructive \"clear\" operation, the demonstrated silent data loss would raise the integrity impact and with it the severity.",
              "fix_verification": "Security closure: rebuilt the library with only `src/coap_net.c` changed (`coap_add_data(new, ...)`), recompiled the PoC against the rebuilt archive, and re-ran it. Before the change the transmitted datagram is `4103e19661b1610162e4000c452e497b` (no payload marker, no `BODY`); after the change it is `41037df061b1610162e4000c70293493ff424f4459` (`0xFF` payload marker followed by `BODY`), and the `coap_add_data: PDU already contains data` warning no longer appears. The trace now shows `:: 'BODY'` on the sent PDU.\n\nBypass review: re-read the diff independently of the analysis that produced it. `coap_get_data(pdu, &data_len, &data)` runs before `coap_add_data(new, ...)`, so `data` still points into the live source PDU while the bytes are copied into `new`; `coap_pdu_duplicate_lkd()` performs a deep copy of option values, and `coap_add_data()` copies the payload, so no pointer into the freed source survives. The payload is added only when the duplicate succeeded, so the `new == NULL` path (unknown abbreviation value, which returns NULL after freeing the duplicate) is unchanged. Checked the sibling call sites of the same pattern: `src/coap_block.c:5197` already targets the duplicated PDU, and `src/coap_net.c` has no other occurrence of this construct. Alternate input classes considered: zero-length payload (`coap_get_data()` returns 0 and nothing is added, unchanged), payload larger than one block (the block layer performs its own correct re-duplication), non-block-mode sessions (the branch is not reached), and sessions where the abbreviation value is not in the mapping table (the duplicate returns NULL, so the send proceeds unexpanded and the payload is untouched).\n\nPreserved behaviour: requests without a Uri-Path-Abbrev option are not affected (the option lookup guards the block), and sessions that never set `no_path_abbrev` bypass the branch entirely, so normal token/option/payload handling is untouched. Ran the project's full unit-test suite against the patched library: 17 suites, 201 tests, 1500 assertions, 0 failures (same result as the unpatched build).\n\nRepository checks: the changed line was compiled with the same flags as the rest of the library and the recompiled archive was linked into both the PoC and the unit-test driver; both link and run cleanly under AddressSanitizer/UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer with no diagnostics. Gap: no test in the suite exercises this branch, so the demonstration and the fixed-versus-unfixed wire comparison are the verification rather than a repository test.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Bind a local UDP socket to act as the upstream CoAP server and note its port.\n2. Create a libcoap client session to that port with `COAP_BLOCK_USE_LIBCOAP` enabled and `session->no_path_abbrev = 1`, which is the state the library itself enters after the server answers `4.02` to a Uri-Path-Abbrev request.\n3. Register a Uri-Path-Abbrev mapping (`coap_upa_server_mapping()`), then build a `PUT` with one Uri-Path-Abbrev option and the 4-byte payload `BODY`, and pass it to `coap_send()`.\n4. Read the datagram that the library actually transmitted.\n5. Observe that the abbreviated option has been expanded to `Uri-Path: a`, `Uri-Path: b`, but the message ends after the options: there is no `0xFF` payload marker and no `BODY`, so the payload was lost.\n\nRunning the same program against a library with `coap_add_data(new, ...)` at the reported line shows the payload present (`... 0x0c 0x70 0x29 0x34 0x93 0xff 'B''O''D''Y'`).",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-770",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unauthenticated memory exhaustion in OSCORE Appendix B.2 context handling\n\nWhen an OSCORE security context has RFC 8613 Appendix B.2 enabled, libcoap derives and permanently links a new OSCORE security context for every incoming request that carries a previously unseen `kid_context`, and it does so **before** the AEAD tag is verified. When verification fails (always, for a peer without the master secret) the newly created context is never removed. An unauthenticated attacker can therefore make the server allocate an unbounded number of security contexts by sending requests with ever-changing `kid_context` values, eventually exhausting memory and taking the CoAP service down."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/coap_oscore.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 944,
                  "endLine": 944,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "oscore_sender_ctx_t *snd_ctx = NULL;"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Track the Appendix B.2 context created for the current request"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/coap_oscore.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 1116,
                  "endLine": 1117,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if (osc_ctx == NULL)\n              goto error;"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Remember the context duplicated for this request"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/coap_oscore.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 1554,
                  "endLine": 1554,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "rcp_ctx->rollback_valid = 0;"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "The duplicated context becomes permanent only after AEAD verification succeeds"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/coap_oscore.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 1863,
                  "endLine": 1865,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if (association && association->is_observe == 0)\n    oscore_delete_association(session, association);\n  coap_delete_pdu_lkd(decrypt_pdu);"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Discard the unauthenticated duplicated context on the failure path"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "CoAP/UDP OSCORE request carrying an OSCORE option with a kid_context",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/coap_oscore.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 944,
                        "endLine": 944
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "oscore_sender_ctx_t *snd_ctx = NULL;\n  oscore_ctx_t *b2_dup_ctx = NULL;"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/coap_oscore.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 1116,
                        "endLine": 1117
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "if (osc_ctx == NULL)\n              goto error;\n            b2_dup_ctx = osc_ctx;"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/coap_oscore.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 1554,
                        "endLine": 1554
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "rcp_ctx->rollback_valid = 0;\n  /* Decryption succeeded - the Appendix B.2 context is now permanent */\n  b2_dup_ctx = NULL;"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/coap_oscore.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 1863,
                        "endLine": 1865
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "if (association && association->is_observe == 0)\n    oscore_delete_association(session, association);\n  if (b2_dup_ctx) {\n    /* Appendix B.2 duplicated a security context for this request, but the\n     * request never authenticated - discard it instead of leaving it linked\n     * to the CoAP context, where it would accumulate on every request. */\n    if (session->recipient_ctx &&\n        session->recipient_ctx->osc_ctx == b2_dup_ctx)\n      oscore_release_recipient_ctx(&session->recipient_ctx);\n    oscore_remove_context(session->context, b2_dup_ctx);\n    b2_dup_ctx = NULL;\n  }\n  coap_delete_pdu_lkd(decrypt_pdu);"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Treat the Appendix B.2 derived security context as provisional until the request that caused its creation has been authenticated: record the context created by the duplication, keep it only when AEAD verification succeeds, and otherwise release the session reference and remove it from the CoAP context chain. Alternatively, rate-limit or cap the number of security contexts that can be created from unauthenticated traffic, or defer context creation until the child context can be validated. Deployments that do not need RFC 8613 Appendix B.2 should keep `rfc8613_b_2` disabled.",
                "markdown": "Treat the Appendix B.2 derived security context as provisional until the request that caused its creation has been authenticated: record the context created by the duplication, keep it only when AEAD verification succeeds, and otherwise release the session reference and remove it from the CoAP context chain. Alternatively, rate-limit or cap the number of security contexts that can be created from unauthenticated traffic, or defer context creation until the child context can be validated. Deployments that do not need RFC 8613 Appendix B.2 should keep `rfc8613_b_2` disabled."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "76584decf4a50c11906ed75d320d7a7e6c48b02dab9622eda8fb0ca4e4a6d372"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.9",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "0cb29b727c2066ad950279e09db8b93fc4e00c7163cc97e4e1dc76ed7e56cbbb",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0002",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.9,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 11:14:54 UTC",
              "target": "libcoap 4.3.5 OSCORE (src/coap_oscore.c, src/oscore/oscore_context.c)",
              "endpoint": "CoAP/UDP OSCORE request carrying an OSCORE option with a kid_context",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-770",
              "impact": "Remote, unauthenticated denial of service. Each crafted datagram permanently adds one OSCORE security context (master secret/salt copies, sender and recipient contexts, derived keys) to the CoAP context chain. Memory grows without bound and is never reclaimed until the process restarts; because context lookup walks the whole chain, request processing cost also degrades quadratically as the chain grows. On the constrained devices libcoap targets, a few thousand packets are enough to exhaust the available heap.",
              "technical_analysis": "Request processing in `coap_oscore_decrypt_pdu()` performs all of the following **before** `cose_encrypt0_decrypt()` is reached:\n\n1. `oscore_decode_option_value()` extracts `kid` and `kid_context` from the OSCORE option.\n2. `oscore_find_context()` fails to match the supplied `kid_context`.\n3. Because the security context has `rfc8613_b_2` set, the code CBOR-unwraps the attacker-supplied `kid_context` and calls `oscore_duplicate_ctx()` (src/coap_oscore.c:1111).\n4. `oscore_duplicate_ctx()` allocates a new `oscore_ctx_t`, copies the master secret/salt, derives a sender key and a common IV, adds a recipient for the supplied `kid`, and links the context into the CoAP context chain with `oscore_enter_context()` (src/oscore/oscore_context.c).\n\nThe AEAD check then fails, and the error path at src/coap_oscore.c:1856-1867 deletes the request association and the PDUs but never unlinks or frees the duplicated context. `osc_ctx` is a local variable that goes out of scope. The context is only ever freed by `oscore_remove_context()`/`coap_free_context()`, so it stays in the CoAP context chain forever.\n\nBecause `oscore_find_context()` iterates the whole context chain for every request, the per-request cost grows linearly with the number of accumulated contexts, so the flood is also a CPU amplification vector.\n\nThe defect is the ordering of \"create security state\" before \"authenticate the message\", combined with a failure path that does not roll the state back. The `kid_context` bytes are fully attacker-controlled (1-250 bytes), so both the number of contexts and their size can be driven by the attacker.",
              "remediation_steps": "Treat the Appendix B.2 derived security context as provisional until the request that caused its creation has been authenticated: record the context created by the duplication, keep it only when AEAD verification succeeds, and otherwise release the session reference and remove it from the CoAP context chain. Alternatively, rate-limit or cap the number of security contexts that can be created from unauthenticated traffic, or defer context creation until the child context can be validated. Deployments that do not need RFC 8613 Appendix B.2 should keep `rfc8613_b_2` disabled.",
              "counterevidence": "I verified the alternative explanation first: the growth is not generic per-packet noise, because the identical flood against the same binary with `rfc8613_b_2,bool,false` leaves resident memory flat (5480 kB to 5580 kB), while the B.2-enabled run grows monotonically in the same test. I also confirmed by walking the context chain that the number of linked OSCORE contexts itself grows by exactly one per request, and that the count stays at one after applying the fix, so the memory is genuinely retained security state rather than allocator slack. Constraints that limit severity are real and are stated in the assumptions: the feature is not enabled by default in the shipped configuration files, and the per-packet cost is modest (tens of bytes for a small `kid_context`, scaling with the attacker-supplied `kid_context` length up to roughly 250 bytes). The time to exhaustion therefore depends on the target's memory budget; on a full-size server it is a slow exhaustion, whereas on a constrained device it is small. I could not test against a real memory-constrained device build.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that a deployed server enables `rfc8613_b_2` (for example an OSCORE interoperability deployment) would raise this to High, because no other precondition would remain between an unauthenticated datagram and unbounded memory growth.",
              "fix_verification": "Gates were worked in order against a build of the patched tree using the same configuration as the original build (OpenSSL backend, COAP_OSCORE_SUPPORT, one OSCORE context with `rfc8613_b_2,bool,true`).\n\n1. Security closure. Re-traced the path: `oscore_duplicate_ctx()` is now recorded in `b2_dup_ctx`; the pointer is cleared only after `cose_encrypt0_decrypt()` returned a positive length and `rcp_ctx->rollback_valid = 0` is executed, so a context survives exactly when the request authenticated. All failure exits between duplication and that point converge on the `error`/`error_no_ack` labels, where the new block releases the session reference and calls `oscore_remove_context()`. Re-ran the chain-walk PoC with the patched build: `contexts before: 1`, `contexts after 2000 unauthenticated requests: 1` (unpatched: 2001). Re-ran the 120,000-datagram flood against a plain (non-sanitizer) build of the patched `coap-server`: resident memory stayed flat, 5516 kB to 5580 kB, instead of growing to 11256 kB.\n\n2. Bypass review. Checked the other callers of `oscore_duplicate_ctx()` (it is called only from this one B.2 branch) and the other exits that follow it: the B.2 step 2 success block deliberately jumps to `error_no_ack` after decryption, which is why the pointer is cleared on the success path instead of relying on the label alone; without that, the legitimate B.2 handshake context would have been destroyed. Also checked the B.2 step 4 path (`session->oscore_r2 != 0`), which updates an existing context and never sets `b2_dup_ctx`. Checked that a session whose `recipient_ctx` does not belong to the duplicated context is left alone by the `session->recipient_ctx->osc_ctx == b2_dup_ctx` guard. Alternate inputs tried: requests with a `kid_context` that fails CBOR unwrapping or exceeds the 255-byte OSCORE-option bound exit before `oscore_duplicate_ctx()` and are unaffected; requests that decrypt successfully leave the context in place; a flood with B.2 disabled is unaffected before and after the change.\n\n3. Preserved behaviour. The successful B.2 path still keeps the derived context, and the failure path still returns NULL after sending the same 4.00/4.01 responses. All 37 OSCORE unit tests pass (`tests/test_oscore.c`, including the RFC 8613 Appendix C test vectors): 37 tests, 389 asserts, 0 failures. The existing 4.01 \"Security context not found\" response for unknown kids is unchanged.\n\n4. Repository checks. The patched tree compiles with no warnings for the changed translation units using the project's own build flags and links cleanly; the OSCORE fuzz harnesses (decrypt with B.2 enabled, inner-plaintext, config parser) run without sanitizer reports against the patched build.\n\nNot executed: a full autotools/cmake build and the complete `make check` suite (the environment provides a hand-rolled build of the library plus the OSCORE test driver). The full non-OSCORE test suite was not run.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Configure an OSCORE security context with `master_secret`, `master_salt`, a `sender_id`, at least one `recipient_id`, and `rfc8613_b_2,bool,true`; start the shipped `coap-server` with that configuration on a local UDP port.\n2. Send a CoAP NON POST datagram to the server. The PDU contains a single OSCORE option (number 9) whose value is `0x19` (Partial IV length 1, H and K bits set), a one-byte Partial IV, the `kid_context` length byte, the CBOR-wrapped `kid_context`, and the recipient key id `0x01`; the payload is arbitrary ciphertext.\n3. Vary the `kid_context` bytes in every datagram so each request names a context the server has not seen before. No valid AEAD tag is ever supplied, so every request is rejected with 4.01.\n4. Observe the server's resident memory grow linearly with the number of packets and never recover; with `rfc8613_b_2,bool,false` the identical traffic leaves the memory flat.\n5. The same effect can be measured deterministically by walking the CoAP context's linked OSCORE context chain and counting the contexts before and after the flood.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-476",
          "ruleIndex": 2,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "NULL pointer dereference in OSCORE external credential lookup crashes the server\n\nWhen OSCORE credentials are supplied through the external credential callback API, `oscore_find_context()` can return a security context while leaving the caller's recipient-context pointer NULL if none of the recipients in the returned credential set matches the key id from the request. The OSCORE request path uses that returned context without checking the recipient pointer and dereferences NULL, crashing the CoAP process. A single unauthenticated OSCORE packet with an attacker-chosen key id triggers it."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/oscore/oscore_context.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 328,
                  "endLine": 328,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "coap_oscore_conf_t *oscore_conf;"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Local holder for the matched recipient"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/oscore/oscore_context.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 371,
                  "endLine": 390,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if (tmp_ctx->recipient_chain != NULL &&\n          tmp_ctx->recipient_chain->next_recipient != NULL) {\n        oscore_recipient_ctx_t *rcp_ctx = tmp_ctx->recipient_chain;\n        oscore_recipient_ctx_t *ref_ctx = NULL;\n        while (rcp_ctx != NULL) {\n          ref_ctx = rcp_ctx;\n          rcp_ctx = rcp_ctx->next_recipient;\n          if (coap_binary_equal(ref_ctx->recipient_id, &rcpkey_id)) {\n            ref_ctx->next_recipient = NULL;\n            *recipient_ctx = ref_ctx;\n          } else {\n            coap_delete_bin_const(ref_ctx->recipient_key);\n            coap_delete_bin_const(ref_ctx->recipient_id);\n            coap_free_type(COAP_OSCORE_REC, (void *)ref_ctx);\n          }\n        }\n      } else {\n        *recipient_ctx = tmp_ctx->recipient_chain;\n      }\n      tmp_ctx->recipient_chain = *recipient_ctx;"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Fail closed when no recipient matches the requested key id"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "CoAP/UDP OSCORE request with an arbitrary OSCORE option key id",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/oscore/oscore_context.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 328,
                        "endLine": 328
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "coap_oscore_conf_t *oscore_conf;\n    oscore_recipient_ctx_t *found_ctx = NULL;"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/oscore/oscore_context.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 371,
                        "endLine": 390
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "if (tmp_ctx->recipient_chain != NULL &&\n          tmp_ctx->recipient_chain->next_recipient != NULL) {\n        oscore_recipient_ctx_t *rcp_ctx = tmp_ctx->recipient_chain;\n        oscore_recipient_ctx_t *ref_ctx = NULL;\n        while (rcp_ctx != NULL) {\n          ref_ctx = rcp_ctx;\n          rcp_ctx = rcp_ctx->next_recipient;\n          if (coap_binary_equal(ref_ctx->recipient_id, &rcpkey_id)) {\n            ref_ctx->next_recipient = NULL;\n            found_ctx = ref_ctx;\n          } else {\n            coap_delete_bin_const(ref_ctx->recipient_key);\n            coap_delete_bin_const(ref_ctx->recipient_id);\n            coap_free_type(COAP_OSCORE_REC, (void *)ref_ctx);\n          }\n        }\n      } else {\n        found_ctx = tmp_ctx->recipient_chain;\n      }\n      if (found_ctx == NULL) {\n        /*\n         * None of the recipients in the supplied credentials match the\n         * requested key id, so there is nothing that can decrypt this\n         * request. Discard the temporary context rather than returning it\n         * with a NULL recipient context to the caller.\n         */\n        tmp_ctx->recipient_chain = NULL;\n        oscore_free_context(tmp_ctx);\n        return NULL;\n      }\n      *recipient_ctx = found_ctx;\n      tmp_ctx->recipient_chain = *recipient_ctx;"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Make the external-credential lookup fail closed: when the credential set returned by the callback contains no recipient matching the requested key id, discard the temporary context and return NULL so the caller answers 4.01 instead of continuing with a NULL recipient context. Applications using the external credential API should also ensure their store returns a credential set that contains the requested recipient id, and should treat unknown key ids as a lookup miss rather than returning a partial context.",
                "markdown": "Make the external-credential lookup fail closed: when the credential set returned by the callback contains no recipient matching the requested key id, discard the temporary context and return NULL so the caller answers 4.01 instead of continuing with a NULL recipient context. Applications using the external credential API should also ensure their store returns a credential set that contains the requested recipient id, and should treat unknown key ids as a lookup miss rather than returning a partial context."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "bf5447a095a1765481839b2516338d63657761b491c1af2252d70d823a8d5b8b"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.9",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "78a5698248f0fdddad7a7cb86a90ab7810afab3f4ef20ace74c4e306966e786a",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0003",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.9,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 11:15:28 UTC",
              "target": "libcoap 4.3.5 OSCORE (src/oscore/oscore_context.c, src/coap_oscore.c)",
              "endpoint": "CoAP/UDP OSCORE request with an arbitrary OSCORE option key id",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-476",
              "impact": "A single unauthenticated datagram can terminate the CoAP server process (NULL pointer dereference), denying service to all clients until it is restarted; libcoap has no isolation between sessions, so the whole service goes down. No credentials or prior state are required, but the target application must be using the external credential callback API with a credential store that can return a recipient set not containing the requested key id.",
              "technical_analysis": "`coap_oscore_decrypt_pdu()` resolves the security context for an incoming request with:\n\n```c\nosc_ctx = oscore_find_context(session, cose->key_id, &cose->kid_context,\n                              NULL, &rcp_ctx);\nif (!osc_ctx) { /* 4.01 */ }\ncoap_oscore_session_set_recipient_ctx(session, rcp_ctx);\nsnd_ctx = osc_ctx->sender_context;\nif ((rcp_ctx->initial_state == 0 || osc_ctx->rfc8613_b_1_2 == 0) && ...)\n```\n\n`oscore_find_context()` has a branch for applications that store credentials externally. The callback returns a `coap_oscore_conf_t`, which is turned into a context. The recipient selection is:\n\n```c\nif (tmp_ctx->recipient_chain != NULL &&\n    tmp_ctx->recipient_chain->next_recipient != NULL) {\n  /* iterate; only assign *recipient_ctx when a recipient id matches */\n} else {\n  *recipient_ctx = tmp_ctx->recipient_chain;\n}\ntmp_ctx->recipient_chain = *recipient_ctx;\n...\nreturn tmp_ctx;\n```\n\nWith two or more recipients and no match, `*recipient_ctx` is never written, so it still holds the caller's initial NULL. The function nevertheless returns the non-NULL context. The recipient's reference count is managed through `*recipient_ctx` only, so the empty temporary context is also left with `recipient_chain == NULL`.\n\nBack in `coap_oscore_decrypt_pdu()`, the NULL `rcp_ctx` is passed to `coap_oscore_session_set_recipient_ctx()` (which returns early for NULL) and then dereferenced by `oscore_validate_sender_seq(rcp_ctx, cose)` at the `rcp_ctx->initial_state` check, i.e. before any AEAD verification. In a normal build this is a read from address 0 and the process dies with SIGSEGV; under UBSAN it is reported as `member access within null pointer of type 'struct oscore_recipient_ctx_t'`.\n\nThe same NULL return is possible for a credential set with no recipients at all, and the shipped `coap-server` can reach this code path with `-O <credential dir>` because it registers `coap_oscore_ctx_find()` as the external find handler and loads whatever configuration file matches the requested key id.",
              "remediation_steps": "Make the external-credential lookup fail closed: when the credential set returned by the callback contains no recipient matching the requested key id, discard the temporary context and return NULL so the caller answers 4.01 instead of continuing with a NULL recipient context. Applications using the external credential API should also ensure their store returns a credential set that contains the requested recipient id, and should treat unknown key ids as a lookup miss rather than returning a partial context.",
              "counterevidence": "The strongest argument against exploitability is the precondition: an application that uses the internal static credential store is unaffected, and an external store that is strictly keyed by the key id will normally return either a matching credential set or no entry at all, in which case the request is rejected correctly. I checked the single-recipient mismatch case as well - there the function returns the (non-NULL) recipient, so decryption simply fails with 4.01 and no crash occurs; the crash needs zero recipients or a multi-recipient set without a match. I also confirmed this is not an artifact of the sanitizer build: a plain build without any sanitizers dies with SIGSEGV (exit 139), while the fixed build answers 4.01 and continues. What remains unverified is how often real deployments populate a credential set for a key id that is not one of its recipients; that depends on the application's storage design and is why the issue is rated Medium rather than High.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Evidence that a deployed libcoap-based server uses external OSCORE credential storage populated per security context (rather than strictly per key id) would raise this to High, since a single unauthenticated packet would then reliably terminate the service.",
              "fix_verification": "Gates worked in order with a patched build of the library (OpenSSL backend, COAP_OSCORE_SUPPORT) linked against a harness that registers an external credential callback.\n\n1. Security closure. `oscore_find_context()` now assigns the selection result to a local `found_ctx` and, when `found_ctx` is NULL, detaches the (empty) recipient chain, frees the temporary context with `oscore_free_context()` and returns NULL. Re-ran the reproduction with the patched build: the request reaches the existing `if (!osc_ctx)` branch, a 4.01 \"Security context not found\" is produced and the function returns NULL; the plain build exits 0 instead of dying with SIGSEGV (exit 139 before the change).\n\n2. Bypass review. Checked the callers of `oscore_find_context()`: `coap_oscore_decrypt_pdu()` (both the request and Appendix B.2 lookups), the response path's association handling (which does not call it) and `coap_persist_observe_add()` in `src/coap_subscribe.c`. The subscribe caller passes the address of a session field, so `*recipient_ctx` is only written when a context was actually found - preserving its previous value otherwise, as before. The single-recipient branch and the matching-recipient branch produce the same values as before (`found_ctx` is then non-NULL and assigned to `*recipient_ctx`), so no behaviour changes for successful lookups. Also checked the zero-recipient case (the `else` branch yields NULL) and the multi-recipient-with-match case.\n\n3. Preserved behaviour. Matching credentials still resolve to the same recipient and context, the `ref` bookkeeping (`already_attached` / decrement) is unchanged, and the 4.01 error response for unknown key ids is the same. All 37 OSCORE unit tests pass (37 tests, 389 asserts, 0 failures), including the RFC 8613 Appendix C vectors.\n\n4. Repository checks. The changed translation unit compiles without warnings with the project's own build flags and links; the OSCORE fuzz harnesses run clean against the patched build.\n\nNot executed: a full autotools/cmake build and the complete `make check` suite; the hand-rolled library build plus the OSCORE test driver was used instead.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Register an external OSCORE credential handler with `coap_oscore_register_external_handlers()` whose find callback returns a security context (master secret, master salt, sender id) containing either no recipients or several recipients, none of which equals the key id in the incoming request.\n2. Create a session toward the local server and build a CoAP NON POST PDU whose OSCORE option (number 9) declares Partial IV length 1, a one-byte Partial IV and the key id `0x01`, followed by an arbitrary payload.\n3. Pass the PDU to `coap_oscore_decrypt_pdu()` (this is what the receive path does for any OSCORE request).\n4. `oscore_find_context()` returns the callback-supplied context with a NULL recipient pointer, and the request path dereferences it in `oscore_validate_sender_seq()`, crashing the process with SIGSEGV.\n5. With the fix, the same input produces a 4.01 \"Security context not found\" response and no crash.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-457",
          "ruleIndex": 3,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Uninitialised heap memory disclosed through re-assembled Block1 body when the block size grows mid-transfer\n\n`libcoap` re-assembles a Block1 request body into a heap buffer but never initialises the bytes the client did not send. A client can force such a gap by increasing the SZX (block size) between blocks: the write offset of the next block is computed from the new SZX (`num << (szx + 4)`), so it jumps past the data already received, while the received-block bookkeeping still treats the transfer as complete. The application is then handed a body of `total_len` bytes in which the gap holds whatever was previously in that heap memory. With the example server's echo mode the leaked bytes are returned to the client in the 2.01 response."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/coap_block.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 3717,
                  "endLine": 3717,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "lg_srcv_is_refed = 1;"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Reject an SZX change once the transfer's block size is established"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/coap_block.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 4594,
                  "endLine": 4596,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if (body_data == NULL && total) {\n    body_data = coap_new_binary(total);\n  }"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Zero the newly allocated body buffer"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/coap_block.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 4620,
                  "endLine": 4624,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "coap_binary_t *new = coap_resize_binary(body_data, offset + length);\n\n    if (new) {\n      body_data = new;\n      memcpy(&body_data->s[offset], data, length);"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Zero the tail added when the body grows"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "CoAP Block1 transfer reassembly (server side), UDP/TCP port 5683",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/coap_block.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 3717,
                        "endLine": 3717
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "lg_srcv_is_refed = 1;\n\n  /*\n   * The SZX (block size) defines the NUM to offset mapping, so it cannot be\n   * changed part way through a reassembled transfer.  A larger SZX makes the\n   * offset of the next NUM jump past the data already received, leaving a gap\n   * in the body that is never filled in but is still reported to the\n   * application as part of the body.  A compliant client keeps using the SZX\n   * that was returned in the 2.31 response.\n   */\n  if (!block.bert && block.num != 0 && block.szx != lg_srcv->szx) {\n    coap_add_data(response, sizeof(\"Block size change\")-1,\n                  (const uint8_t *)\"Block size change\");\n    response->code = COAP_RESPONSE_CODE(408);\n    goto free_lg_srcv;\n  }"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/coap_block.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 4594,
                        "endLine": 4596
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "if (body_data == NULL && total) {\n    body_data = coap_new_binary(total);\n    if (body_data)\n      memset(body_data->s, 0, body_data->length);\n  }"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "src/coap_block.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 4620,
                        "endLine": 4624
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "size_t old_length = body_data->length;\n    coap_binary_t *new = coap_resize_binary(body_data, offset + length);\n\n    if (new) {\n      body_data = new;\n      if (body_data->length > old_length)\n        memset(&body_data->s[old_length], 0, body_data->length - old_length);\n      memcpy(&body_data->s[offset], data, length);"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Reject a Block1/Q-Block1 request whose SZX differs from the block size already established for that transfer (except for the first block), because the NUM to offset mapping of an in-progress transfer is fixed and a size increase leaves a gap that is never filled. Additionally, never expose never-written bytes: zero the body buffer when it is created and zero the newly added tail whenever the body is grown, so that a gap is defined data rather than heap contents. Both changes are needed — the first enforces the RFC 7959 block-sequence rule, the second guarantees the \"every byte of the delivered body was either supplied by the client or is zero\" invariant for any future path that computes an offset.",
                "markdown": "Reject a Block1/Q-Block1 request whose SZX differs from the block size already established for that transfer (except for the first block), because the NUM to offset mapping of an in-progress transfer is fixed and a size increase leaves a gap that is never filled. Additionally, never expose never-written bytes: zero the body buffer when it is created and zero the newly added tail whenever the body is grown, so that a gap is defined data rather than heap contents. Both changes are needed — the first enforces the RFC 7959 block-sequence rule, the second guarantees the \"every byte of the delivered body was either supplied by the client or is zero\" invariant for any future path that computes an offset."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "d4d6769a81e4ad63783e244c34575ddefe0995ae33126afa3aa610d10ca6b046"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "ba7a0c59f262a3bc9cb9b66af3b2fac9080cd03406baf936dbcbfe2554841747",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0004",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 11:22:31 UTC",
              "target": "libcoap 4.3.5 (source snapshot), src/coap_block.c",
              "endpoint": "CoAP Block1 transfer reassembly (server side), UDP/TCP port 5683",
              "method": "CoAP (PUT with Block1 option)",
              "cwe": "CWE-457",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated client that can send UDP or TCP CoAP requests obtains heap contents it never supplied: on a non-sanitised build, 38 of 40 attempts returned previously freed request payloads and heap/library pointers inside the 2.01 response body, and the same buffer is handed to any application using `COAP_BLOCK_SINGLE_BODY` or the public `coap_block_build_body()` helper. The leaked region is attacker-sized (up to the configured maximum body size) and repeatable, so it can be used to read freed heap data such as other peers' request payloads and pointer values that help defeat ASLR. Integrity and availability are not affected by the PoC.",
              "technical_analysis": "Two library routines combine to produce the disclosure.\n\n**1. Offset derived from the per-request SZX.** In `coap_handle_request_put_block()` the transfer's block size is recorded once, when the reassembly state (`lg_srcv`) is created:\n\n```c title=src/coap_block.c startLineNumber=3693 endLineNumber=3697\n    max_block_szx = COAP_BLOCK_MAX_SIZE_GET(session->block_mode);\n    if (!block.bert && block.num == 0 && max_block_szx != 0 &&\n        max_block_szx < block.szx) {\n      lg_srcv->szx = max_block_szx;\n    } else {\n      lg_srcv->szx = block.szx;\n    }\n```\n\nbut the offset of every block is computed from the SZX carried in that individual request:\n\n```c title=src/coap_block.c startLineNumber=3601 endLineNumber=3601\n  offset = block.num << (block.szx + 4);\n```\n\nand again while marking the logical blocks as received:\n\n```c title=src/coap_block.c startLineNumber=3787 endLineNumber=3790\n    block.num++;\n    offset = block.num << (block.szx + 4);\n```\n\nNothing checks that `block.szx` still equals the established `lg_srcv->szx`, so a request sequence of `NUM=0/SZX=0` (16 bytes at offset 0) followed by `NUM=1/SZX=6` (1024 bytes at offset 1024) is accepted. `blocks_add_entry()` records NUM 0 and NUM 1, `check_all_blocks_in()` therefore reports the transfer as complete, and the body is passed to the application with `body_length = lg_srcv->total_len` (2048 in the example), although offsets 16..1023 were never written. RFC 7959 2.2/2.4 only permits the server to reduce the block size and requires the NUM/offset mapping to stay consistent, so this sequence is exactly the kind of malformed input a parser must reject.\n\n**2. New buffer space is not initialised.** `coap_block_build_body_lkd()` allocates the body with `coap_new_binary()`, which allocates `sizeof(coap_str_bin_union_t) + size` bytes and does not clear the data area, then only `memcpy()`s the bytes of the current block into it:\n\n```c title=src/coap_block.c startLineNumber=4594 endLineNumber=4596\n  if (body_data == NULL && total) {\n    body_data = coap_new_binary(total);\n  }\n...\n    coap_binary_t *new = coap_resize_binary(body_data, offset + length);\n    if (new) {\n      body_data = new;\n      memcpy(&body_data->s[offset], data, length);\n```\n\nThe same function is public (`coap_block_build_body()`) and is the documented way for applications to re-assemble a body, so applications outside the library inherit the same uninitialised bytes. In the shipped example server the `-L 1` (default, application re-assembly) path uses this helper, and the `-L 3` (`COAP_BLOCK_SINGLE_BODY`) path receives the library's `lg_srcv->body_data` directly as `pdu->body_data`, so both configurations are affected.\n\nThe combination is confirmed by ASan, which reports the gap as its own uninitialised-malloc pattern (`0xbe`), and by a plain `RelWithDebInfo` build, which returns previously freed payloads and pointer values.",
              "remediation_steps": "Reject a Block1/Q-Block1 request whose SZX differs from the block size already established for that transfer (except for the first block), because the NUM to offset mapping of an in-progress transfer is fixed and a size increase leaves a gap that is never filled. Additionally, never expose never-written bytes: zero the body buffer when it is created and zero the newly added tail whenever the body is grown, so that a gap is defined data rather than heap contents. Both changes are needed — the first enforces the RFC 7959 block-sequence rule, the second guarantees the \"every byte of the delivered body was either supplied by the client or is zero\" invariant for any future path that computes an offset.",
              "counterevidence": "Checked for a control that would stop the leak before the body is handed out: (1) `coap_get_block_b()` only enforces SZX != 7 and NUM <= 0xFFFFF, no cross-request consistency; (2) the existing `lg_srcv->total_len_set` guard only rejects blocks that reach *past* the last block, and the growing-SZX block is the last block itself, so it passes; (3) `blocks_add_entry()`/`check_all_blocks_in()` track NUM values only, never byte coverage, so a gap is indistinguishable from a complete body; (4) no memset of the body buffer exists on either the allocation or the growth path, and `coap_new_binary()` clears only the header, not the data area. Tested both the `-L 3` (library re-assembly) and `-L 1` (application re-assembly through the public helper) configurations, and both a sanitised and a plain build to exclude an ASan-only artefact. The only precondition is a client that is non-compliant (RFC 7959 requires the SZX to be reduced only by the server and the NUM/offset mapping to stay consistent), which is not a mitigation because the client is the attacker. Residual limitation: the amount of disclosure depends on what the allocator hands back; on a freshly grown heap the gap may be zeros, which is why the report demonstrates it with heap reuse (38/40) rather than on the first request.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Demonstrating that a long-lived secret (for example DTLS PSK material, OSCORE keys or another peer's full request body) is present in the disclosed heap region would raise this to high severity (C:H); conversely, if the delivered body is only ever consumed without being stored, echoed or acted upon, the impact reduces to uninitialised-memory use with no observable disclosure and the finding drops to low.",
              "fix_verification": "**1. Security closure.** Patched copy of the tree built out-of-tree (`RelWithDebInfo`, no sanitizer, `-DENABLE_DTLS=OFF`) and exercised with the same PoC.\n* `-L 3` (library re-assembly): the second request is now answered `4.08` with the diagnostic \"Block size change\" instead of `2.01` plus a sparse body; the re-assembly state is dropped, so the client can restart. Grooming runs: 0/40 iterations produced any leaked byte (unpatched: 38/40).\n* `-L 1` (application re-assembly through the public `coap_block_build_body()`): 0/40 leaked iterations (unpatched: 26/40); the gap now contains only zero bytes.\n* Re-traced the sink: with the SZX check in place the only way bytes reach the body is a block whose SZX matches the established one, and with the zero-fill any remaining unwritten byte is 0, so `pdu->body_data[0..total_len-1]` can no longer contain heap contents.\n\n**2. Bypass review.** (a) Sibling call sites of `coap_block_build_body_lkd()`: the server Block1 path (`coap_block.c:3860`) and the client Block2 path (`coap_block.c:4971`) both inherit the zero-fill, so a Block2 response with a sparse/overlapping NUM sequence is covered as well. (b) Other offsets computed from an attacker-supplied SZX: `offset = block.num << (block.szx + 4)` at lines 3601 and 3787 — the first is the non-re-assembly path, where the library hands each block to the application and cannot know the application's buffer; that path is now bounded by the zero-filled public helper for the applications that use it, which the `-L 1` test confirms. (c) Alternate malformed inputs tried: a *decreasing* SZX mid-transfer (`NUM=0/SZX=6` then `NUM=1/SZX=2`) tiles the already-written region and stays accepted (correct); `block.num == 0` restarts and retransmissions are still accepted, because the check is scoped to `num != 0`; BERT is excluded (`block.bert`), and BERT offsets already advance in fixed 1024-byte steps. (d) Q-Block1 uses the same routine and the same offset expression, so it is covered by the same check.\n\n**3. Preserved behaviour.** A compliant transfer (`NUM=0/SZX=2/M=1` then `NUM=1/SZX=2/M=0`, 64+64 bytes) still completes with `2.01` and the correct 128-byte body; the server-side SZX reduction path (first block larger than the configured maximum, `lg_srcv->szx` clamped, client renumbering to the returned SZX) still matches the check because it compares against the clamped value; error semantics for the existing rejects (`Missing interim block`, `Block after last block`, `Content-Format mismatch`) are unchanged.\n\n**4. Repository checks.** `tests/testdriver` built from the patched tree and run to completion: 18 suites, 203 tests, 0 failures, 1500 assertions passing (DTLS disabled in that build, so the 19 `t_tls*` tests are absent; the same suite passes 240/240 in the DTLS-enabled sanitizer build of the unpatched tree). The patched library also compiles cleanly with the project's default warning set. Gap: the DTLS-enabled sanitizer build was not rebuilt from the patched tree, so the fix has not been exercised together with the GnuTLS backend or against the interoperability examples (`etsi_iot_01`, `coap-client`).",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Start the shipped example server with echo mode and library-side single-body re-assembly on a loopback port: `coap-server -A 127.0.0.1 -p 15689 -e -d 10 -L 3`.\n2. From a single UDP socket (so both requests belong to the same session), send a Block1 PUT to `/test` with `NUM=0`, `SZX=0` (16 bytes), `M=1` and a 16-byte payload. The server answers 2.31 Continue with `Block1:0/M/16`.\n3. From the same socket send the final block with `NUM=1`, `SZX=6` (1024-byte block unit), `M=0` and a 64-byte payload. The offset of this block is computed as 1 << 10 = 1024 while the first block ended at 16.\n4. The server answers 2.01 Changed. Because `-e` is set, the response body is the re-assembled 128-byte body with the block option removed.\n5. Observe that bytes 16..63 of the response body were never sent by the client. On an ASan build they contain the allocator's `0xbe` uninitialised pattern; on a normal build they contain stale heap data (see the grooming variant below).\n6. Repeat steps 2-3 after a completed 128-byte transfer of a known pattern: previously freed payload bytes and pointer values appear in the gap in most attempts.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/memory-safety-in-pre-auth-packet-parsing-oob-read-write-size-t-underflow",
          "ruleIndex": 4,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Memory safety in pre-auth packet parsing (OOB read/write, size_t underflow) — Ruled out: src/coap_pdu.c + src/coap_option.c (option iterator, extended delta/length, extended token, UDP/TCP/WS header parse)\n\nEncode/decode symmetry checked against coap_opt_setheader/coap_add_token/coap_pdu_encode_header (13->+13, 269->+269, e_token_length bias 1/2, COAP_TOKEN_EXT_2B_BIAS 269, block-num limit 0xFFFFF) and every per-option length limit in coap_pdu_parse_opt_base matches RFC 7252/7959/8323/8613/8768/9455. Length control is ADVANCE_OPT/ADVANCE_OPT_CHECK plus `if (length < result->length) return 0` in coap_opt_parse (src/coap_option.c:41-107), which runs on every attacker-reachable option before the value is used. 1.6M ASAN fuzz execs over pdu_parse_udp/tcp/ws with no finding."
          },
          "locations": [
