        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/memory-exhaustion-allocation-amplification-via-attacker-controlled-size1-or-block-number",
          "ruleIndex": 25,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Memory exhaustion / allocation amplification via attacker-controlled Size1 or block number — Ruled out: src/coap_block.c Size1 / total_len driven reassembly allocation\n\nMeasured directly: a 1024-byte Block1 packet with Size1=0x10000000 (256MB) against the ASAN test server increased RSS by only 6.4MB (27804kB -> 34424kB) for an accepted 2.31 response, because coap_new_binary() is followed by a sparse memcpy of just the received block; pages are only committed as blocks actually arrive. Peak accepted body is capped at MAX_BLK_LEN (1GB at szx=6, src/coap_block.c:895) and the write is bounded by coap_resize_binary(offset+length), so the attacker must send as much data as they allocate. Failure of a large allocation is handled (500 \"Memory issue\"). No amplification beyond bytes sent, so not a viable DoS vector."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "src/coap_block.c Size1 / total_len driven reassembly allocation"
                }
