        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/integer-truncation-in-var-byte-codecs-used-on-wire-option-values",
          "ruleIndex": 29,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Integer truncation in var-byte codecs used on wire option values — Ruled out: src/coap_encode.c coap_decode_var_bytes / coap_encode_var_safe / coap_encode_var_safe8 / coap_fls(ll)\n\ncoap_decode_var_bytes (src/coap_encode.c:38) reads exactly the caller-supplied len into an unsigned int; every wire call site passes a length that coap_pdu_parse_opt_base already bounded for that option (<=4 for uint options, <=2 for Content-Format/Accept) or uses coap_decode_var_bytes8 which stops at min(len, sizeof(uint64_t)) (src/coap_encode.c:70). coap_encode_var_safe/safe8 (54, 74) return 0 when the encoded width exceeds the supplied buffer instead of writing past it, and callers (e.g. src/coap_pdu.c:454 coap_add_token, src/coap_block.c:711) size their buffers deliberately (uint8_t[8] with sizeof(uint32)/uint64). The size_t/unsigned truncations I looked for - option length into uint16_t seg_len in coap_get_uri_path/coap_get_query - cannot be reached with a length > 255 for Uri-Path/Uri-Query/Uri-Path-Abbrev because coap_pdu_parse_opt_base caps them at 1338/1352 and the option is rejected (402) before dispatch, and allocation and write loops use the same truncated value regardless. 2.4M invariant-checked parses over UDP/TCP/WS plus 1.9M libFuzzer execs of the pdu_parse harnesses found no length disagreement."
          },
