        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/wrong-layer-dispatch-e-g-ws-handshake-before-tls-leading-to-parsing-on-an-unestablished-transport",
          "ruleIndex": 30,
          "kind": "notApplicable",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Wrong-layer dispatch (e.g. WS handshake before TLS) leading to parsing on an unestablished transport — Not applicable: src/coap_layers.c layer function-pointer table (COAP_PROTO_* x COAP_LAYER_*)\n\ncoap_layers.c contains no parsing or arithmetic, only the dispatch table, so a memory-safety defect cannot sit there; I instead verified the table is semantically correct for the establish chain: coap_session_check_connect/accept call lfunc[COAP_LAYER_SESSION].l_establish, which is coap_session_establish for UDP/TCP, coap_tls_establish for TLS/WSS (starts the TLS handshake) and coap_ws_establish for WS; the TLS backends call lfunc[COAP_LAYER_TLS].l_establish on handshake completion, which is coap_ws_establish for WSS and NULL for TLS (src/coap_openssl.c:4039 etc.), and coap_ws_establish completes to lfunc[COAP_LAYER_WS].l_establish = coap_session_establish - so for WSS the order is TLS -> WS -> CoAP CSM. Verified end-to-end: coap+ws:// and coap+tcp:// GETs against the local server returned the resource list, and the debug log shows \"WS: established\" followed by \"sending CSM\"."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
