        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/security-context-selection-semantics-id-context-downgrade-zero-length-id-context",
          "ruleIndex": 48,
          "kind": "open",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Security-context selection semantics / ID-context downgrade (zero-length id_context) — Requires further review: src/oscore/oscore_context.c oscore_find_context non-callback path + oscore_duplicate_ctx zero-length id_context\n\nOpen item. With rfc8613_b_2 enabled, a request whose CBOR kid_context is an empty byte string makes oscore_duplicate_ctx() store a zero-length id_context (coap_new_bin_const with length 0 yields a non-NULL object whose s pointer is NULL). Later lookups then compare it with memcmp(pt->id_context->s, ctxkey_id->s, 0), which UBSAN reports as \"null pointer passed as argument 2\" at src/oscore/oscore_context.c:426; a zero-length id_context also compares equal to any request that carries no kid_context, so the duplicated context can be selected for kid-only lookups. In a normal build memcmp with a zero length is harmless and selecting the context still requires the AEAD check to pass with that context's derived keys, so I could not demonstrate a security consequence (confidentiality/integrity/availability) and did not file it. Gap: whether an attacker who legitimately holds the master secret can use the zero-length context to reach a state the protocol forbids (e.g. an ID-context downgrade) was not established."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "src/oscore/oscore_context.c oscore_find_context non-callback path + oscore_duplicate_ctx zero-length id_context"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "strix": {
