        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/integer-truncation-of-the-option-length-leading-to-undersized-allocation",
          "ruleIndex": 51,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Integer truncation of the option length leading to undersized allocation — Ruled out: uint16_t seg_len truncation in coap_get_query()/coap_get_uri_path()\n\nSpecific candidate: `uint16_t seg_len = coap_opt_length(q)` (src/coap_uri.c:1124 and :1277) truncates the uint32_t maximum option length (65804). Tested by hand-building wire PDUs that bypass the public API caps and feeding them to coap_pdu_parse (UDP and TCP), with declared option lengths 65534/65535/65536/65537/60000/65799/65804 (/workspace/uria/h2.c). For 65536 the truncated value is 0 and the returned string is 0 bytes; for 65804 it is 268 and the returned string is 268 encoded bytes; in every case returned length == bytes actually written and s[length] == NUL, with no ASAN/UBSAN report - because both passes truncate identically, so the allocation and the writes still agree. The truncation is data loss, not memory unsafety, and it is unreachable anyway: coap_pdu_parse_opt_base rejects every Uri-Path/Uri-Query option longer than 255 bytes (src/coap_pdu.c:1336-1341 and :1359-1362), called from every builder (coap_insert_option :711, coap_update_option :815, coap_add_option_internal :872) and from the parse path (:1511). Confirmed on the network path: an oversized option gets 4.02 'Bad Option' and is never dispatched (src/coap_net.c:3187-3199), so coap_get_uri_path()/coap_get_query() never see a >255 segment."
          },
          "locations": [
