        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/cache-key-semantic-confusion-from-a-non-injective-uri-option-encoding",
          "ruleIndex": 54,
          "kind": "open",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Cache-key / semantic confusion from a non-injective URI option encoding — Requires further review: '&'/'='/'/' literal-byte handling in the coap_get_query()/coap_get_uri_path() encoder\n\ncoap_get_query()/coap_get_uri_path() escape every non-RFC3986-char byte but treat '&', '=' and '/' (query) plus '/' (path) as literal \"unescaped\" bytes (src/coap_uri.c:1083-1107), so the encoding is not injective: one Uri-Query option with value `a&b` and two options `a`,`b` both assemble to `a&b`; a Uri-Path value containing '/' assembles indistinguishable from two segments. Consequences that would matter: src/coap_block.c:788-790 and :2691 use the assembled query string as a Block2/observe large-body cache key, and src/coap_proxy.c:923 passes it as the cache key for proxied bodies, so two different option sets can share a cache entry even though the application handler sees different option objects. I could NOT demonstrate the impact: the example resources are query-insensitive, and building a query-sensitive handler plus a Block2 cache-collision scenario was outside this task's assigned functions. I also could not name a control that prevents collision - the encoding simply has no canonicalisation. Note this behaviour is inherited from upstream libcoap (same is_unescaped_in_path/is_unescaped_in_query definitions), i.e. not introduced by the recent comments/patch in these functions."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "'&'/'='/'/' literal-byte handling in the coap_get_query()/coap_get_uri_path() encoder"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
